The alternative method is to assign a name to the previously bound `Pet` py::class_ object and reference it when binding the `Dog` class, passing it as the parent class_ object:

```cpp
py::class_<Pet> pet(m, "Pet");
pet.def(py::init<const std::string &>())
   .def_readwrite("name", &Pet::name);

// Method 2: pass parent class_ object:
py::class_<Dog>(m, "Dog", pet /* <- specify Python parent type */)
   .def(py::init<const std::string &>())
   .def("bark", &Dog::bark);
```

This approach specifies the Python parent type by passing the `pet` object directly, rather than using the C++ base class as a template parameter.
